<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Bowen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Bowen was a British colonial governor who became the first governor of Queensland when it separated from New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> in 1859. He later governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, Victoria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>. In Queensland he presided over rapid pastoral expansion and the setting up of the colony's parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-bowen.jpg" alt="George Bowen" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Bowen_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Ferguson Bowen was born on 2 November 1821, the eldest son of the Rev. Edward Bowen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> rector of Taughboyne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a>, and probably grew up at Bogay House near Newtown Cunningham. He was educated at Charterhouse School and Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, took a first in classics in 1844, was twice President of the Oxford Union and became a fellow of Brasenose College. In 1847 he became president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-academy">Ionian Academy</a> at Corfu and in 1854 chief secretary to the government of the Ionian Islands, where in 1856 he married Contessa Diamantina di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, daughter of the President of the Ionian Senate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sworn in as governor of Queensland on 10 December 1859, he had more influence than other Australian governors, largely through Robert Herbert, who came with him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and became the colony's first premier. He promoted exploration and a volunteer force, and though his refusal to allow inconvertible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> in the financial crisis of 1866 was unpopular, the colonists asked for his term to be extended. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> he saw the end of the New Zealand Wars and created the New Zealand Cross for colonial soldiers, for which he was reprimanded before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> upheld it.</p><p>Moved to Victoria in March 1873, he faced the deadlock between the Legislative Council and the Legislative Assembly. In January 1878, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a>'s advice, he agreed to Premier Graham Berry's mass dismissal of public servants on Black Wednesday, a decision he said had damaged his career, though Hugh Childers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a> approved it. He governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> from 4 April 1879 to 9 December 1880 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> from 30 March 1883, where his administration set up the Hong Kong Observatory, a government hospital and the Causeway Bay typhoon shelter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ill health ended his governorship in 1887, after which he led a Royal Commission that drafted a new constitution for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and was sworn into the Privy Council. He wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and published Thirty Years of Colonial Government in 1889. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamantina">Diamantina</a>'s death in 1893 he married Letitia Florence White. He died of bronchitis in Brighton on 21 February 1899 and was buried at Kensal Green. The Queensland town of Bowen, Bowen Hills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> and streets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Bowen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The district opened to free settlement in 1842 and was part of the Colony of New South Wales until 6 June 1859, when it became a separate colony with <b>George Bowen</b> as governor and Brisbane as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-queensland">Colony of Queensland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Brighton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.92,-8&amp;year=1871" aria-label="County Donegal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4374 285 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Bowen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4824 555h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4824 555h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.92,-8&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
